this video is for students who are  thinking of applying to Oxford or  Cambridge their teachers parents and  guardians now if you're any one of those  then you probably have a huge number of  questions right now about the  application procedures and about the  universities in general and I really  hope this video is going to go at least  some way to answering your questions now  while this video is going to provide you  with some simple answers to your  questions its real purpose is to direct  you to the other fantastic information  sources which are available online with  so many available it's difficult to know  where to start and so I really hope that  this video will point you in the right  direction as a rule of thumb both Oxford  and Cambridge have fantastically  informative websites they are Oh X dot a  C dot uk' and cam dot a C dot u K  respectively and all the links which are  appearing up here will also be down  there in the description firstly some  basic questions and first of all why  should I want to apply to Oxford or  Cambridge well the simple answer to this  is that both Oxford and Cambridge are  world leading institutions which offer  highly competitive degree programs if  you were to graduate from either one of  them then you'll be highly sought after  in the global job market and would be  very well equipped to deal with pretty  much any situation that your field could  throw you there are of course many other  reasons why you want to apply the  collegiate system allows for smaller  tight-knit communities than non  collegiate universities the tutorial or  supervision system allows for an  unparalleled level of student academic  contact and both universities have  fantastic alumni networks which allow  you to connect and give you  opportunities that are just not  available elsewhere as well as the  fantastic extracurricular scene at both  universities choosing between the two  universities as you must do for your  first undergraduate degree is  fundamentally a personal choice informed  by differences in the courses  differences in the towns that they  inhabit and numerable small factors the  best advice I can give you is to  carefully consider what you want to get  out of your degree and if that does not  the question for you then to visit both  of the towns Oxford is more of a city  whereas Cambridge is more of a market  town feel and they suit different people  all else being equal just choose where  you would prefer to call home for three  or four years all this being said Oxford  and Cambridge are not for everyone  I'd ask you to carefully consider all  the information that's available online  and make a decision about whether it is  the right place for you there are many  other Russell Group universities offer  similarly rigorous and competitive  degree programs in a less intense way  with longer terms and if you feel  like that might be more suited to you  than do not feel pressured into applying  to universities where you do not think  you will be happy  secondly what the Oxford and Cambridge  look for in their students now this  question has a very simple answer the  admittance tutors are looking for the  brightest the most capable students in  their fields there are a huge number of  myths and misconceptions that people  believe about Oxford and Cambridge and  the fact of the matter is that both  universities simply want the best of  brightest students this can be  demonstrated by the student in two ways  firstly academic excellence cheaters  will be looking for students with the  best grades possible and they will  almost exclusively be concerned with  grades in subjects directly related to  the degree you're applying for though  this is covered in more detail later  secondly cheaters will look for students  who are passionate about their subject  this can be demonstrated in many ways  through clubs through reading through  taking your understanding of the subject  beyond what's required of you at school  the ideal student will be someone who  has excelled in their field or in fields  closely related to it in the case of  subjects that aren't studied at school  and has taken their understanding of  their subject beyond what is asked of  them off their own back in both academic  and extracurricular excellence  independent thoughts and ingenuity are  very highly prized by the cheaters so  try and encourage this and cultivate it  wherever you can for more information on  what the university is officially look  for they have official web pages on this  subject here and here for Oxford and  Cambridge thirdly we'll my background or  school change how likely it is that I  will get in as your answer this question  is know one of the most persistent myths  that people believe are Oxbridge is that  only rich public school educated kids  can get in and this is categorically not  true  I for example went to Oxford in 2009  study physics and I went to a state  comprehensive school when I was the  first member of my family to go to  university my background simply wasn't  an issue the tutors didn't care about it  all they cared about was my ability to  do my subject I actually made an entire  video on the subject of whether Oxbridge  admissions are fair and you can check  that out here there is however a caveat  to this question  Oxford uses a flagging system whereby  students who are from underperforming  schools who are in care or from deprived  postcodes are much more likely to be  invited to interview for more  information about  scheme check out the link here and for  information about a very similar  cambridge scheme check the link here  lastly for a discussion about what  Oxford has been doing to improve access  to students from a typical backgrounds  check out this interview I did with one  of my colleges access reps here fourth  question how do I choose a course in  many cases the subject that you'll be  applying for chooses itself I for  example applied for physics because it  was the subject that I was most  passionate about but in some cases there  are subject which are very similar that  you might be undecided about for example  physics engineering and maths or there  might be subjects which aren't offered  in schools like archaeology and  anthropology in these cases it is highly  advisable to do your research lists of  courses available at Oxford and  Cambridge can be found here and here  respectively and each department at the  university has its own website which can  be either found from the main University  page or from a quick Google search I'd  recommend that you check out every  departments website that you are  interested in possibly applying to and  in addition to these websites that are  also prospectus is available which are  either electronically available online  or can be requested in print form when  checking for this information I highly  recommend that you look at the course  structure the material covered and the  method of assessment before making a  firm decision ultimately choosing a  course is a highly personal decision but  you can make this decision as informed  as possible and try to make sure that  you can see yourself successfully and  happily studying your subject for three  four or even more years fifth question  what is the college system and how do I  choose a college now the college system  is one of the most confusing aspects of  Oxbridge to applicants and for good  reason it's pretty much unique in global  education each University is broken down  into multiple colleges and these exist  separately from the department system  each College provides accommodation  pastoral support and other resources to  its students and typically houses a few  hundred postgraduate and undergraduate  students some teaching is done by the  college for example your tutorials or  provisions in first-year will most  likely be at your college while the  majority the teaching for example of  your lectures and any lab work will be  coordinated by the department the  question of which course to choose is an  immensely difficult one and there is no  one right answer but in a way there's  also no wrong answer because every  student ends up thinking that their  college is the best in the university so  godless of what you choose you'll end up  being happy and proud firstly they'll  find out which colleges offer your  subject as not all subjects are offered  at all colleges for Oxford you can find  this out here of Cambridge you can find  this out  here then ask yourself what kind of  college you would like to live in  ultimately your college is going to be  your home for three or four years and  personally I think that the most  important criterion in choosing is where  you feel comfortable and home so with  that in mind I personally think the best  way to choose a college is to physically  go to Oxford or Cambridge and visit the  colleges you are interested in applying  to while there you can pick up  documentation like prospectuses you can  get a tour of the grounds and a chance  to just drink in the atmosphere often  the choice makes itself before going  though you might want to whittle down  all the available options into a  shortlist in which case consider the  following questions does the size of the  college matter to you would you prefer  to live in a big one or a small one does  the age or the grandeur of the college  matter  would you like guaranteed accommodation  for your entire degree would you like to  live out for some of it and do college  sports matter to you or music or the  Arts each College has its own website  and social media accounts and so you can  use these to get a good sense of what  life is like at these colleges though  this aspect is caring more fully at the  end of the video lastly if all of this  sounds like too much work  then you can always submit in an open  application and the university will  choose a college for you though I'd at  least recommend investigating your  options actually to return to a point I  made earlier about every student  thinking their colleges are the best in  university the best College in Oxford is  in fact some pieces College Oxford more  information about which can be found at  SBC oxa CDOT UK did I just show some  buyers
 

